  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mostly dry and comfortable weather is expected for \nLabor Day Weekend. The exception to this will be later tonight \ninto Sunday when some showers could affect the New Jersey \ncoastal plain as well as parts of Delmarva. \n\nMainly dry weather is expected to prevail for the day today as \nhigh pressure slowly builds in to our north. One thing to note \nthough is that a broad upper level trough will be lingering to \nour east over the Canadian Maritimes and this will still be \nsending spokes of energy around it into the mid Atlantic region.\nFor this reason, can't completely rule out some spotty showers \nmainly over Delmarva. However even here POPs are only 20% or \nless so most areas should not see anything. Otherwise, following\nsome early morning stratus generally expect a mix of sun and \nclouds with highs generally in the low to mid 80s except upper \n70s north. It will also be notably less humid and more \ncomfortable compared to our recent weather. \n\nFor tonight into the first half of Sunday, another spoke of \nenergy rounding the upper low to our east will bring a somewhat \nbetter chance for some showers to affect the NJ coastal plain\ninto portions of Delmarva. This will also result in some \ncloudiness overnight into the day Sunday. This combined with NE \nwinds increasing to 10-15 mph into the day Sunday along the \ncoast will result in not quite as ideal conditions there as \nearlier forecast. Expect highs Sunday generally only in the mid \n70s near the coast due to the onshore flow with upper 70s to low\n80s inland. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Minor tidal flooding is anticipated with Sunday \nafternoon's high tide, mainly for the Jersey Shore, Delaware \nBeaches, and Delaware Bay communities.\n\nFlow will turn more onshore over the next day or so. This will \nresult in water piling up over consecutive tide cycles, potentially \nleading to minor tidal flooding across portions of our area on \nSunday afternoon. The current forecast has minor tidal flooding from \nBarnegat Bay on south along the Atlantic Ocean coastline and for \nareas within Delaware Bay. Advisories may be needed in these spots \nbut will wait to see how things trend over the 12 hours with the \nforecast.\n\nWithin the tidal Delaware River, northern NJ coast, and Chesapeake \nBay, tidal flooding will be limited to spotty minor or no flooding \nat all. Tide levels should decrease getting into next week as flow \nturns more offshore.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A cold front will pass through on Thursday bringing \nthe potential for another round of showers and storms.\n\nBy Tuesday, high pressure will begin to shift offshore welcoming the \nreturn of slightly above-normal temperatures and an increase in \nhumidity levels. High pressure looks like it will still be in \ncontrol through mid-week, before giving way to a cold front that \nwill be approaching from the north and west Wednesday night into \nThursday. This front will be the next opportunity for any showers or \nthunderstorms around the area. However moisture with the front could \nend up being limited so at this point POPs are only around 20 to 30 \npercent or less.",
